</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Bryan Kian Hsiang Low
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>School of Computing
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>National University of Singapore
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_email"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Email: </span><a href="mailto:"><span id="id4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">lowkh@comp.nus.edu.sg</span></a>
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Large language models (LLMs) have demonstrated promise in emulating human-like responses across a wide range of tasks. In this paper, we propose a novel alignment framework that treats LLMs as agent proxies for human survey respondents, affording a cost-effective and steerable solution to two pressing challenges in the social sciences: the rising cost of survey deployment and the growing demographic imbalance in survey response data. Drawing inspiration from the theory of revealed preference, we formulate alignment as a two-stage problem: constructing diverse agent personas called <span id="abstract1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">endowments</span> that simulate plausible respondent profiles, and selecting a representative subset to approximate a ground-truth population based on observed data. To implement the paradigm, we introduce <span id="abstract1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">P2P</span>, a system that steers LLM agents toward representative behavioral patterns using structured prompt engineering, entropy-based sampling, and regression-based selection. Unlike personalization-heavy approaches, our alignment approach is demographic-agnostic and relies only on aggregate survey results, offering better generalizability and parsimony. Beyond improving data efficiency in social science research, our framework offers a testbed for studying the operationalization of pluralistic alignment. We demonstrate the efficacy of our approach on real-world opinion survey datasets, showing that our aligned agent populations can reproduce aggregate response patterns with high fidelity and exhibit substantial response diversity, even without demographic conditioning.</p>
  
</div>
<div id="id5" class="ltx_logical-block ltx_align_bottom">
    <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:100%;height:2.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span>
    <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:100%;height:2.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span>
    
<div id="p1" class="ltx_para">
<table id="p1.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_top">
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="p1.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><span class="ltx_rule" style="width:0.0pt;height:24.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"></span></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>
  
</div>
<div id="p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="p2.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">Keywords</em> Preference modeling  <math id="p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\cdot" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⋅</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\cdot</annotation></semantics></math>
Value alignment  <math id="p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\cdot" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⋅</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\cdot</annotation></semantics></math>
Evaluation</p>
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Pollsters are facing mounting challenges in the contemporary landscape, as public willingness to participate in surveys declines across regions and over time. In the United States, telephone response rates have dropped from 36% in the late 1990s to under 6% today <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kennedy (2019)</a></cite>, while major federal surveys such as the Current Population Survey (CPS) have experienced a 20-point drop since 2015 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">U.S. Bureau of Labor Statistics (2025)</a></cite>. Likewise, across Europe <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jabkowski and Cichocki (2025)</a></cite>, response rates for national surveys in 36 European countries have fallen 0.8% per year on average between 1999 and 2018. This erosion of participation undermines statistical power and representativeness, particularly because the decline is not uniform across demographic groups. For instance, recent election polling failures have been traced to differential nonresponse: Republicans and independents are increasingly less likely to answer than Democrats <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Clinton et al. (2022)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Parshall (2024)</a></cite>. In the social sciences, this is a classic case of <span id="S1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">sample selection bias</span>, which, if left unchecked, compromises both predictive accuracy and external validity of the survey-based inference. At the same time, the exorbitant cost of conducting large-scale surveys, due to respondent recruitment, compensation, and extensive piloting, places constraints on experimentation and iteration <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kennedy (2015)</a></cite>. These challenges highlight a pressing need for scalable, flexible, and theoretically grounded alternatives for modeling human preferences.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The epistemic challenge of capturing human preferences, long studied in the social sciences, now finds a structural parallel in large language model (LLM) alignment, where researchers aim to steer model behavior in line with human values and beliefs. Standard approaches, such as supervised fine-tuning (SFT) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tan et al. (2024)</a></cite> and reinforcement learning from human feedback (RLHF) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bai et al. (2022)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Christiano et al. (2017)</a></cite>, train models to reflect normative ideals of helpfulness, harmlessness, or truthfulness. While effective for building general-purpose assistants, these techniques are inherently monolithic: they produce a single dominant behavioral profile, making them ill-suited for modeling pluralistic human populations. Scaling them to reflect multiple value systems requires retraining or fine-tuning for each group, rendering the approach costly, brittle, and inflexible. Consequently, these pipelines struggle with tasks that demand population-level diversity, such as simulating public opinion or generating group-sensitive predictions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">To address this rigidity, alignment researchers have increasingly turned to post-training conditioning techniques that guide model behavior at inference time. A prominent example is the use of structured prompts, variously termed endowments <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Horton (2023)</a></cite>, personas <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Castricato et al. (2025)</a></cite>, or Silicon Samples <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Argyle et al. (2023)</a></cite>, that encode sociodemographic attributes or behavioral dispositions. Prompted this way, LLMs can emulate individual-level responses with considerable fidelity <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bisbee et al. (2024)</a></cite>, making them attractive for constructing synthetic populations to pilot surveys or test hypotheses. However, this approach remains limited in key respects. First, it depends heavily on human-curated prompts or ground-truth demographic profiles, which may be unavailable or incomplete. Second, it often produces synthetic responses that lack the natural diversity of real populations: LLMs exhibit central-category bias <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al. (2024)</a></cite> and generate insufficient inter-agent variation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bisbee et al. (2024)</a></cite>. Thus, while endowments reduce the need for retraining, they increase reliance on demographic templates and fall short of capturing true representational diversity.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">These limitations reveal a common assumption in alignment: that behavioral fidelity hinges on demographic accuracy. We challenge this view and propose a new theoretical framework grounded in <span id="S1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">preference reconstruction</span>. Rather than aligning each synthetic agent to a ground-truth demographic profile, we argue that effective emulation requires only the recovery of the aggregate preference structure of the target population. Drawing on revealed preference theory in economics, we treat each agent as a proxy decison-maker whose responses expose latent beliefs or utilities. Crucially, we do not seek to recover the <span id="S1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">true</span> basis of individual human preferences. Instead, it suffices to identify a <span id="S1.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">functional basis</span> in the latent preference space, a diverse set of proxy agents that span the opinion manifold, and determine appropriate aggregation weights over them. This abstraction is reflected in human societies: demographically distinct populations may exhibit similar collective attitudes, even if their constituent beliefs diverge. Our approach thus enables a more data- and alignment-efficient route to survey emulation, moving beyond identity-conditioned generation toward flexible distributional modeling.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">This perspective also clarifies and concretizes the goals of pluralistic alignment <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sorensen et al. (2024)</a></cite>, which advocates representing a spectrum of human values rather than optimizing for a singular objective. While compelling in principle, the pluralistic alignment literature has remained largely agnostic about how to measure pluralism or evaluate pluralistic systems. Our framework addresses this gap by proposing concrete criteria for measuring variation and fidelity, along with a mechanism to steer agent distributions efficiently under resource constraints so that the aggregate distribution of answers across the agent ensemble matches that of a given target population. In doing so, we operationalize distributional pluralistic alignment through a lens grounded in economic theory and suited for practical survey applications.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Our contributions are as follows:</p>
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theoretical foundation.</span> We formalize preference alignment as a two-stage process: constructing a functional basis of proxy agents and determining aggregation weights, drawing on revealed preference theory to recover population-level preferences.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Modular alignment system.</span> We implement this theory via a modular system, Prompts to Proxies (<span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">P2P</span>), that combines active endowment generation (to promote response diversity) with regression-based aggregation (to estimate preferences without demographic alignment).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Pluralistic alignment in practice.</span> We provide the first operational framework for pluralistic alignment, introducing metrics for diversity and fidelity and propose scalable strategies to balance representational coverage and resource efficiency.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Bridge to empirical social science.</span> Our framework enables social scientists to simulate and test preference theories by controlling agent endowments and tracking their effects on response distributions. It also supports survey design and can mitigate nonresponse bias, offering a cost-effective complement to traditional data collection.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Testbed for alignment research.</span> The modular design provides a controlled environment for evaluating prompting strategies, enabling alignment researchers to explore steerability and value-sensitive modeling at scale.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Modeling Preferences via Functional Bases</h2>

<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Preference modeling under conventional lenses</h5>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Human preferences are latent, complex, and often inconsistent, posing a persistent challenge for formal modeling. Economists and social scientists have approached this challenge through multiple modeling frameworks. One prominent approach models humans as rational agents who maximize a stable utility function. While elegant in theory and mathematically tractable, these models often struggle to explain empirical anomalies in observed behavior <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kahneman and Tversky (1979)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Thaler (1999)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kahneman and Tversky (2000)</a></cite>. A complementary framework, revealed preference theory <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Samuelson (1938)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mas-Colell et al. (1995)</a></cite>, shifts the focus from explicit utility functions to observable choices. It asks whether a pattern of choices can be rationalized as if generated by a utility-maximizing agent without assuming a specific functional form. This idea has proven especially valuable in empirical settings, offering a way to assess whether behavior is consistent with stable preferences directly from observed data.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We take inspiration from the revealed preference perspective—not in its full rationality axioms, but in its core idea: preferences, though hidden, can be inferred from behaviors, such as responses to survey questions. This principle also underlies modern alignment pipelines such as SFT and RLHF, where models are steered toward human-preferred outputs using behavioral signals in the form of labeled data or feedback on generated responses.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Our framework</h5>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To formalize our approach, we zoom out from individual preferences and return to the level of survey analysis. Surveys are not designed to recover each respondent’s utility function; rather, they aim to capture aggregate population preferences, often through summary statistics such as response distributions or marginal means. This shift in perspective from individual to population invites a functional view (Figure <a href="#S2.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ Our framework ‣ 2 Modeling Preferences via Functional Bases ‣ Prompts to Proxies: Emulating Human Preferences via a Compact LLM Ensemble" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>): we can represent the population preference as a linear combination of individual preference vectors in a latent preference space. Responses to survey questions can then be viewed as mappings from this latent space to an observable response space. Crucially, this mapping is generally <span id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">many-to-one</span>: different underlying preferences may yield identical observed responses. Yet, as the number and diversity of responses increase, the expressive capacity of the response space improves. In the limit, a sufficiently rich battery of responses enables an increasingly precise identification of latent preferences. This perspective motivates our framework: Instead of recovering the ground-truth preference of each individual, we can construct a <span id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">functional basis</span>, a diverse set of proxy agents whose preferences span the latent space. By observing their responses and estimating the appropriate aggregation weights, which can be positive or negative, we can reconstruct the aggregate preference structure of the population.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S2.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.11311v1/figures/preference_model.png" id="S2.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:286/213;" width="286" height="213" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span><span id="S2.F1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Top</span>: Finding a functional basis to recover latent ground-truth preference. <span id="S2.F1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Bottom</span>: Identification of latent preference through multiple observations.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Alignment as a two-stage problem</h5>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Based on our theoretical framework, we can cast alignment as a two-stage problem. The first stage involves constructing a broad and expressive candidate basis of proxy agents, each representing a distinct vector in the latent preference space. For the basis to be effective, it must capture meaningful variation across the population. This objective aligns closely with recent calls for <span id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">pluralistic alignment</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sorensen et al. (2024)</a></cite>, which stresses the importance of representing a spectrum of human values rather than converging on a single ideal. However, we do not have direct access to individual preferences, and the prompt space is far too vast to explore in an unstructured manner. To address this, we adopt a structured prompting strategy based on <span id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">attributes</span>—interpretable factors whose variation is likely to influence preference expression. These attributes may be informed from theory (e.g., ideology, values, levels of altruism) or content (e.g., topical relevance). By combining and varying these attributes systematically, we generate a set of endowment-conditioned agents that span different positions in the preference space.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The second stage concentrates on designing an aggregation mechanism to reconstruct the population preference from proxy agents. We frame this as a supervised learning problem: given aggregate responses for a subset of survey questions, we estimate a set of aggregate weights over the proxy agents based on their individual response patterns. These weights are then used to predict responses on held-out questions, enabling out-of-sample evaluation. This setup allows us to examine whether the weighted agentic ensemble indeed captures the target population preference, rather than overfitting to the training set. We adopt regression-based methods for this purpose, as they align naturally with our assumption that population preferences emerge as linear combinations of individual vectors in the latent space. We describe implementation details, including attribute construction, endowment generation, and constrained regression methods, in the following section.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Prompts to Proxies (<span id="S3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">P2P</span>)</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To implement our preference reconstruction strategy in the survey context, we engineer a modular alignment system called <span id="S3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Prompts to Proxies (<span id="S3.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">P2P</span>)</span> (Figure <a href="#S3.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 3 Prompts to Proxies (P2P) ‣ Prompts to Proxies: Emulating Human Preferences via a Compact LLM Ensemble" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>). The system consists of two core components: (1) active endowment generation, powered by a dynamic attribute bank, and (2) regression-based aggregation, where constrained variable section algorithms form the backbone. They concretize the two stages of alignment introduced in the previous section and enable the generation of a compact set of opinionated LLM agents to emulate the results of observed population-level surveys.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.11311v1/P2P_illustration.png" id="S3.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/240;" width="476" height="240" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span><span id="S3.F2.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">System Overview of the Prompts to Proxies (<span id="S3.F2.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">P2P</span>) Framework.</span> <span id="S3.F2.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">P2P</span> operationalizes preference alignment as a two-stage process inspired by revealed preference theory. In <span id="S3.F2.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Stage 1</span>, active endowment generation uses a structured attribute bank and entropy-guided adaptive sampling strategies to construct diverse agent personas, called endowments. In <span id="S3.F2.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Stage 2</span>, regression-based aggregation assigns weights to reconstruct population-level preference patterns. An external validity check accesses how well the weighted ensemble predicts aggregate responses on held-out questions, assessing out-of-sample generalization.</figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Survey Splitting and Evaluation Protocol</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To support the alignment procedure, we partition the full set of survey questions, denoted <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>, into three disjoint subsets: <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Q}_{\mathrm{train}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mi>train</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Q}_{\mathrm{train}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Q}_{\mathrm{valid}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mi>valid</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Q}_{\mathrm{valid}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Q}_{\mathrm{test}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mi>test</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Q}_{\mathrm{test}}</annotation></semantics></math>, corresponding to the training, validation, and test sets, respectively. Let <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=|\mathcal{Q}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=|\mathcal{Q}|</annotation></semantics></math> denote the total number of questions, and <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{\mathrm{train}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>train</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{\mathrm{train}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{\mathrm{valid}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>valid</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{\mathrm{valid}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{\mathrm{test}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>test</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{\mathrm{test}}</annotation></semantics></math> the cardinalities of the three subsets. <span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">P2P</span> allows users to set the split sets and ratio flexibly.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For the alignment system, <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Q}_{\mathrm{train}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mi>train</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Q}_{\mathrm{train}}</annotation></semantics></math> is used for active endowment generation. <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Q}_{\mathrm{train}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mi>train</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Q}_{\mathrm{train}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Q}_{\mathrm{valid}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mi>valid</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Q}_{\mathrm{valid}}</annotation></semantics></math> are used in regression-based aggregation. We reserve <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Q}_{\mathrm{test}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mi>test</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Q}_{\mathrm{test}}</annotation></semantics></math> as an ultimate test of the predictive accuracy of the selected agent ensemble, ensuring that the reconstructed preference has a decent degree of generalizability. For regression analysis, categorical questions are binarized into question-option pairs.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Attributes as Control Handles</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As a crucial part of the active endowment generation logic, we use attributes to shape model preferences. This approach, though counter-intuitive at first glance, is in fact a deliberate reverse engineering design choice. In humans, attributes such as ideology, dispositions, or values are better understood as reflections or downstream manifestations of latent preferences. However, language models do not possess preferences in the human sense. As next-token prediction machines, their behavior is shaped by statistical associations learned from human text corpora. This distinction allows us to invert the causal direction: rather than treating attributes as reflections of internal preference states, we tap into the pattern matching nature of LLMs and use attributes as <span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">control handles</span> to steer model behavior.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To ensure that attributes effectively steer model preferences, we design a preset <span id="S3.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">attribute bank</span> that draws inspiration from theories across diverse social science disciplines, such as economics, political science, management science, and psychology. The bank is organized hierarchically: individual attributes are grouped into <span id="S3.SS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">modes</span>, each representing a coherent subject area (e.g., values and beliefs, cognitive biases, or political ideology). These modes are further clustered into higher-level <span id="S3.SS2.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">templates</span>, which reflect different design purposes or theoretical orientations.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We define three primary templates. The <span id="S3.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">core</span> template contains general demographic descriptors and serves as the default baseline. The <span id="S3.SS2.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">thematic</span> template includes topic-specific modes such as economics, politics, culture and history. The <span id="S3.SS2.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">theoretical</span> template groups attributes derived from canonical frameworks such as Maslow’s hierarchy of needs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Maslow (1943)</a></cite> and the Big Five personality traits <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">John and Srivastava (1999)</a></cite>. A condensed summary of these templates, representative modes, and attributes is shown in Table <a href="#S3.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 3.2 Attributes as Control Handles ‣ 3 Prompts to Proxies (P2P) ‣ Prompts to Proxies: Emulating Human Preferences via a Compact LLM Ensemble" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>. These templates and modes are not fixed: they serve as a structured starting point but are fully extensible. Users can define new templates, introduce domain-specific modes, or modify existing attribute sets to suit different experimental contexts. By sampling and varying attributes from this flexible bank, we create prompts that instantiate distinct proxy agents occupying different regions of the latent preference space.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.T1" class="ltx_table">
<table id="S3.T1.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
