<span id="S3.T1.2.3.1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.T1.2.3.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Thematic</span></span>
</span></td>
<td id="S3.T1.2.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_left ltx_align_top ltx_border_t">
<span id="S3.T1.2.3.2.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:170.7pt;">
<span id="S3.T1.2.3.2.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.T1.2.3.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">economics</span>: occupation, price sensitivity, wealth/debt, <span id="S3.T1.2.3.2.1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">…</span></span>
</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T1.2.4" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S3.T1.2.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top ltx_border_r"></td>
<td id="S3.T1.2.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_left ltx_align_top">
<span id="S3.T1.2.4.2.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:170.7pt;">
<span id="S3.T1.2.4.2.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.T1.2.4.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">politics</span>: policy preferences, civic engagement, ideological orientation, <span id="S3.T1.2.4.2.1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">…</span></span>
</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T1.2.5" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S3.T1.2.5.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top ltx_border_r"></td>
<td id="S3.T1.2.5.2" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_left ltx_align_top">
<span id="S3.T1.2.5.2.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:170.7pt;">
<span id="S3.T1.2.5.2.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.T1.2.5.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">behavioral_biases</span>: status quo bias, loss aversion, overconfidence bias, <span id="S3.T1.2.5.2.1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">…</span></span>
</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T1.2.6" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S3.T1.2.6.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">
<span id="S3.T1.2.6.1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:56.9pt;">
<span id="S3.T1.2.6.1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.T1.2.6.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theoretical</span></span>
</span></td>
<td id="S3.T1.2.6.2" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_left ltx_align_top ltx_border_t">
<span id="S3.T1.2.6.2.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:170.7pt;">
<span id="S3.T1.2.6.2.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.T1.2.6.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">maslow</span>: safety needs, self-actualization, love and belonging needs, <span id="S3.T1.2.6.2.1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">…</span></span>
</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T1.2.7" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S3.T1.2.7.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top ltx_border_b ltx_border_r"></td>
<td id="S3.T1.2.7.2" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_left ltx_align_top ltx_border_b">
<span id="S3.T1.2.7.2.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:170.7pt;">
<span id="S3.T1.2.7.2.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.T1.2.7.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">big_five</span>: conscientiousness, neuroticism, extraversion, <span id="S3.T1.2.7.2.1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">…</span></span>
</span></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 1: </span>Structured view of the attribute bank, with representative modes and attributes grouped by template. Users can extend the bank with extra templates, modes, or attributes.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">While the attribute bank provides structured coverage, we also enable dynamic attribute discovery. We introduce two additional <span id="S3.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">freeform templates</span> that are specific to the survey under analysis. The <span id="S3.SS2.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">survey</span> template derives attributes by prompting a designated LLM, called <span id="S3.SS2.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">attribute learner</span>, to identify relevant decision-making factors based on the overall survey topic covered by the training set of questions, <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Q}_{\mathrm{train}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mi>train</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Q}_{\mathrm{train}}</annotation></semantics></math>. The <span id="S3.SS2.p4.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">question</span> template goes further by asking the learner to derive attributes tailored to a specific survey question. In both cases, the inferred attributes are appended to the attribute bank only for the duration of the survey run. This hybrid strategy strikes a balance between theory-driven and data-driven endowment generations, and enables human-AI co-discovery of salient preference descriptors.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">With the attribute bank in place, we build an endowment generator using a dedicated LLM termed <span id="S3.SS2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">endowment model</span> that takes a set of attributes as input and produces diverse agent profiles by varying the instantiation of each selected attribute.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3 </span>Active Endowment Generation</h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To construct a diverse preference basis without exhaustively enumerating attribute combinations, we create an active endowment generation procedure to operate under resource constraints. It proceeds in iterative steps. At each step, new endowments are sampled, instantiated as agents to elicit responses to survey questions, and evaluated based on their contribution to response diversity. The remainder of this section describes the key components of this procedure, including our variability scoring strategy, adaptive sampling steps, and entropy-based patching mechanisms.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Tracking response diversity with variability score</h5>

<div id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As established in Section <a href="#S2" title="2 Modeling Preferences via Functional Bases ‣ Prompts to Proxies: Emulating Human Preferences via a Compact LLM Ensemble" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, observed responses can serve as behavioral manifestations for latent preference vectors. Through this link, response variability serves as an initial diagnostic for assessing diversity in the underlying preference space. To effectively measure response variability, we introduce the notion of <span id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">question entropy</span>:</p>
<table id="S3.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{i}(\mathcal{A})=\frac{-\sum_{k=1}^{K_{i}}p_{ik}\log_{2}p_{ik}}{\log_{2}K_{i}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">−</mo><mo>∑</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msup><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mi>log</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{i}(\mathcal{A})=\frac{-\sum_{k=1}^{K_{i}}p_{ik}\log_{2}p_{ik}}{\log_{2}K_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{i}(\mathcal{A})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{i}(\mathcal{A})</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the normalized entropy of question <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> based on responses from a group of agents <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is the number of unique response options <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{1,\dots,K_{i}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{1,\dots,K_{i}\}</annotation></semantics></math> for question <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{ik}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{ik}</annotation></semantics></math> is the empirical proportion of responses selecting option <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>. The denominator normalizes for scale, ensuring that entropy is comparable across questions with different numbers of choices. A high question entropy indicates greater response diversity for that question. By varying the group <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}</annotation></semantics></math>, e.g., restricting it to agents sampled from a particular mode, we can compute question entropy at the group level and compare the diversity contributes across modes.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To turn question entropy into a useful metric for adaptive sampling, we introduce <span id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">variability score</span> for each mode. Formally, it is defined as</p>
<table id="S3.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V(\mathcal{A}_{\mathrm{mode}})=\sum_{i\in\mathcal{Q}_{\mathrm{train}}}\frac{H_{i}(\mathcal{A}_{\mathrm{mode}})}{H_{i}(\mathcal{A}_{\mathrm{total}})}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>mode</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mi>train</mi></msub></mrow></munder><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>mode</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>total</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V(\mathcal{A}_{\mathrm{mode}})=\sum_{i\in\mathcal{Q}_{\mathrm{train}}}\frac{H_{i}(\mathcal{A}_{\mathrm{mode}})}{H_{i}(\mathcal{A}_{\mathrm{total}})}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{i}(\mathcal{A}_{\mathrm{mode}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>mode</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{i}(\mathcal{A}_{\mathrm{mode}})</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the entropy of responses to question <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> based on the cumulative set of agents from a given mode up to the current generation step, and <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{i}(\mathcal{A}_{\mathrm{total}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>total</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{i}(\mathcal{A}_{\mathrm{total}})</annotation></semantics></math> is the corresponding question entropy across all agents generated so far.
Computed this way, the variability score favors modes that are capable of generating endowments which elicit diverse responses to questions that other modes typically fail to diversify. The intuition is to maximize the informational utility of each observation under budget constraints. When most agents agree on a question, a divergent response signals a valuable distinction in the underlying preference space. In <span id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">P2P</span>, we task a dedicated class object, <span id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Tracker</span>, to compute the variability score for each mode after each generation round. The scores are then passed through a softmax function to produce a probabilty distribution over modes, which is used to guide endowment sampling in the next generation step.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Adaptive sampling and update steps</h5>

<div id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In <span id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">P2P</span>, endowment generation takes several steps. At the outset of the generation, an attribute learner is invoked to infer attributes from the survey based on the training questions, yielding the survey template on top of the existing templates in the attribute bank. The system then enters an initial sampling stage, drawing an equal number of endowments from each mode. The endowments are then used to instantiate agents to elicit responses to training questions, <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Q}_{\mathrm{train}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mi>train</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Q}_{\mathrm{train}}</annotation></semantics></math>. The collective responses are then passed to <span id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Tracker</span> for the computation of the variability scores and the sampling probabilities for the next update step. At the next update step, endowment budget allocation follows the multinomial distribution. A higher probability exploits the top-performing mode, while randomness allows exploration of its under-performing peers.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Patching low-entropy questions</h5>

<div id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In addition to the existing trade-off between exploitation and exploration, we adopt an additional boosting strategy, which we call <span id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">question patching</span>. At the end of each update step, as the <span id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Tracker</span> computes the variability scores for each existing mode, it also keeps track of the questions with the lowest entropy values. When question patching is activated, the attribute learner will be called in the next generation step to exclusively infer attributes on each of the lowest-<math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> entropy questions, where <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> is user-defined. The split of the endowment budget between the active generation and boosting generation is governed by a user-specified ratio.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F3" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.11311v1/figures/entropy_change_heatmap.png" id="S3.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:429/96;" width="429" height="96" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3: </span>Entropy change across update steps for each question during active endowment generation for ATP W42. Each column represents a question. Green indicates an increase in entropy from the previous step, while red indicates a decline.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To avoid wasting resources on persistently low-entropy and to promote cross-pollination between modes, we additionally introduce a <span id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">mixed mode</span> strategy. It is triggered after a question appears <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> times in the lowest-<math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> entropy list, where <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> is a user-defined threshold. When in effect, the strategy relocates the assigned endowment budge to a new mixed mode, which combines attributes from the question with those of the current top-performing mode. This encourages diversity injection from high-performing modes while maintaining a question-specific focus.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The adaptive sampling cycle continues until the generation budget <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{A}</annotation></semantics></math> (an upper bound on the maximum number of agents allowed) is exhausted. By iteratively targeting low-diversity questions and expanding the attribute space, active endowment generation progressively increases response diversity while preserving coverage over previously explored regions of the preference space.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.4 </span>Regression-Based Aggregation</h3>

<div id="S3.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">With the candidate proxy basis assembled, <span id="S3.SS4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">P2P</span> enters the second stage: Regression-based Aggregation. The goal at this stage is to find a functional basis from the candidate vectors to reconstruct the observed preference. Depending on the endowment budget and the total number of trainable questions <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Q}_{\mathrm{train}}\cup Q_{\mathrm{valid}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mi>train</mi></msub><mo>∪</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>valid</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Q}_{\mathrm{train}}\cup Q_{\mathrm{valid}}</annotation></semantics></math>, we could end up in a situation where there are more variables than observations. Consequently, a variable selection algorithm is vital. Besides, even with <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{\mathrm{train}}+N_{\mathrm{valid}}&gt;&gt;N_{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>train</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>valid</mi></msub></mrow><mo>&gt;&gt;</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{\mathrm{train}}+N_{\mathrm{valid}}&gt;&gt;N_{A}</annotation></semantics></math>, variable selection allows us to retain a parsimonious agent ensemble, saving inference costs on further simulations. To conduct variable selection, we consider regression methods with an added L1 penalization term. We can use either hold-out validation or cross-validation (CV) for hyperparameter tuning. The objective of the learning problem can be stated as</p>
<table id="S3.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E3.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\min_{\mathbf{w}}\sum_{i\in\mathcal{Q}_{\mathrm{train}}}\sum_{k=1}^{K_{i}}\left(r_{ik}-\sum_{j=1}^{N_{A}}w_{j}d_{j,ik}\right)^{2}+\alpha\Omega(\mathbf{w})," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder><mi>min</mi><mi>𝐰</mi></munder><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mi>train</mi></msub></mrow></munder></mstyle><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></munderover></mstyle><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></munderover></mstyle><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mi>α</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐰</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\min_{\mathbf{w}}\sum_{i\in\mathcal{Q}_{\mathrm{train}}}\sum_{k=1}^{K_{i}}\left(r_{ik}-\sum_{j=1}^{N_{A}}w_{j}d_{j,ik}\right)^{2}+\alpha\Omega(\mathbf{w}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.E4"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathrm{s.t.}\quad\sum_{k=1}^{K_{i}}\sum_{j=1}^{N_{A}}w_{j}d_{j,ik}=1\quad\forall i\in\mathcal{Q}_{\mathrm{train}}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo><mspace style="width:1.167em;" width="1.167em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></munderover></mstyle><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mspace style="width:1.167em;" width="1.167em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mi>train</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathrm{s.t.}\quad\sum_{k=1}^{K_{i}}\sum_{j=1}^{N_{A}}w_{j}d_{j,ik}=1\quad\forall i\in\mathcal{Q}_{\mathrm{train}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS4.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{w}\in\mathbb{R}^{N_{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{w}\in\mathbb{R}^{N_{A}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the weight vector for the agents, <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{ik}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{ik}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the ground-truth proportion of responses selecting option <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> for question <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{j,ik}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{j,ik}</annotation></semantics></math> is a dummy response variable equal to <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> if agent <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> selects option <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> otherwise, and <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> is the hyperparameter for the penalization. When <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega</annotation></semantics></math> consists of only a L1 norm, the formulation amounts to constrained lasso. When <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega</annotation></semantics></math> subsumes an additional L2 norm, it becomes constrained elastic net. Our simulation studies indicates that constrained lasso faithfully recovers the ground-truth agents from a large candidate pool under an adequate number of observations (Appendix A.1), and it is potent in selecting a proxy ensemble to recover ground truth even when the number of proxy agents is small (Appendix A.2). Both results corroborate using it as a selection backbone. Our simulation study also investigates the relationship between entropy and prediction fit—we leave the details to Appendix A.3 for interested readers.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Taking <span id="S4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">P2P</span> to the Wild</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As a first empirical test of the alignment framework, we deploy <span id="S4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">P2P</span> on real-world social survey data. Specifically, we use the American Trends Panel (ATP), a nationally representative U.S. survey conducted by Pew Research Center in discrete waves. In this demonstration, we choose Wave 42, which probes trust in science by asking respondents about scientific competence, integrity, and public accountability. The wave includes responses from approximately 4464 U.S. adults <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Pew Research Center (2019)</a></cite> and the cleaned dataset contains 128 questions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For the experiment, we split the questions according to a <math id="S4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="7:1.5:1.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mn>1.5</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mn>1.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">7:1.5:1.5</annotation></semantics></math> ratio into the training, validation and test sets. For the endowment generation stage, we set the endowment budget to be <math id="S4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="300" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>300</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">300</annotation></semantics></math>, with <math id="S4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>10</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10</annotation></semantics></math> endowments generated for each mode during initial sampling. Subsequently, the rest of the endowments are generated across 5 update steps. In each step, we allocate 75% of the endowment budget to question patching, where the lowest 3 questions are considered. We set the trigger threshold of the mixed mode strategy at 1, so that the budget is reallocated if a question enters the lowest-3 list the second time. At the end of the generation, 5 questions are patched, with additionally 5 mixed modes created.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Endowment generation improves entropy</h5>

<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#S3.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ Patching low-entropy questions ‣ 3.3 Active Endowment Generation ‣ 3 Prompts to Proxies (P2P) ‣ Prompts to Proxies: Emulating Human Preferences via a Compact LLM Ensemble" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> displays the entropy change for each question across the update steps. Evidently, a significant number of questions experience a noticeable rise in entropy at the end of the first update step. From the second step onward, mixed mode enters the generation loop. Marginal gains in entropy are observed for the majority of questions, whereas some experience mild entropy drops. Appendix B provides question entropy trajectories in levels. The three questions that exhibit the largest drops in entropy are among the highest-entropy questions at the initial sampling stage. The evolution elucidates the tradeoff made by the active endowment generator encoded in the variability scores.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For the selection of the agent ensemble, we fit both a constrained lasso and a constrained elastic net, using CV to select the penalization parameters. Consequently, lasso selects 53 out of the 300 synthetic agents to form the agent ensemble, while elastic net selects 43. The training and testing performances are similar between the two methods.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F4" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.11311v1/figures/prediction_results.png" id="S4.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:286/313;" width="286" height="313" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 4: </span>Prompts to Proxies: Emulation Results for ATP W42. <span id="S4.F4.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Top</span>: Train (left) and test (right) prediction performances for the binarized responses. <span id="S4.F4.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Bottom</span>: Human and agent (pre- and post- alignment) aggregated responses for the sample test question <span id="S4.F4.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">RQ4_F1Ae_W42</span>.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Prediction indicates feasibility</h5>

<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#S4.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ Endowment generation improves entropy ‣ 4 Taking P2P to the Wild ‣ Prompts to Proxies: Emulating Human Preferences via a Compact LLM Ensemble" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> shows the prediction performances of the agent ensemble selected with constrained lasso. The train-time root mean squared error (RMSE) is 0.08, with test RMSE slightly higher at 0.09, indicating adequate generalization ability of the functional basis. The bottom panel of Figure <a href="#S4.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ Endowment generation improves entropy ‣ 4 Taking P2P to the Wild ‣ Prompts to Proxies: Emulating Human Preferences via a Compact LLM Ensemble" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> presents a snapshot for the aggregated responses for a test set question, comparing the human ground truth with agentic emulations before and after regression-based aggregation. Overall, the results are encouraging, especially considering that <span id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">P2P</span> constructs the endowments based solely on preset and learned attributes and is agnostic on the ground-truth demographic data.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">The panoramic view and future directions</h5>

<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In Appendix B, we provide a preliminary panel study, applying <span id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">P2P</span> to 14 ATP waves, each repeated 3 times. Performance variation is relatively low within each wave but considerably higher across the panel. In 8 out of 14 waves, <span id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">P2P</span> consistently achieves a test MSE below 0.015 across all repeats. Four waves exhibit test MSEs fluctuating around 0.015, while the remaining two fall in the range of 0.020 to 0.025. While our simulation studies suggest that entropy coverage, the number of observations and the size of the proxy pool all influence emulation performance, the preliminary panel results signal intriguing directions for future study. Understanding the sources of variation in empirical performance—particularly in relation to both the similarities and the distinctions from simulation results—offers a promising lens on the dynamics of human–AI alignment. These insights can also inform refinements to <span id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">P2P</span> ’s modular architecture and motivate extensions of its functionality.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph"><span id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">P2P</span> as a means to an important end</h5>

<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">At a broader level, our framework operationalizes pluralistic alignment <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sorensen et al. (2024)</a></cite>. Its structured use of attribute-based control enables the representation of diverse value systems, while its regression-based weighting mechanism supports distributional pluralism without enforcing demographic fidelity. Crucially, <span id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">P2P</span> demonstrates that steerability and pluralism are not separate challenges but deeply intertwined: the ability to steer model behavior through promptable attributes is what enables meaningful diversity in aggregate preference reconstruction.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Although developed in the context of survey emulation, <span id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">P2P</span> generalizes naturally beyond surveys. The underlying preference reconstruction logic can be applied to other forms of observational data by discretizing continuous variables and binarizing categorical ones. Moreover, while this paper focuses on modeling aggregate population preferences, the framework could be adapted for individual-level alignment—for example, by learning proxy ensembles that emulate preferences of a single person across varied contexts.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Beyond its methodological contributions, <span id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">P2P</span> affords a modular platform for empirical alignment. Our work is among the first to streamline the use of large-scale social survey data—not as textual corpora for language model pretraining, but as a structured, theory-informed substrate for steering models toward specific populations, cultural milieus, and value contexts. It supports systematic evaluation of prompt engineering techniques, attribute design strategies and sampling heuristics. Researchers can test how different endowment structures affect response diversity, and how entropy metrics reflect or obscure latent preference variation. These tools offer a bridge between high-level alignment goals and grounded behavioral evaluation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Finally, <span id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">P2P</span> offers a testbed for social scientists to probe and operationalize preference theories. By investigating how assumptions about cognitive style, value orientation, or belief structure affect response distributions and mode-level entropy, researchers can explore the explanatory power to theoretical constructs in a controlled but flexible setting. In this sense, the system fulfills the spirit of its name: <span id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">P2P</span> connects peer disciplines, translates prospects into products, carries ideas from pseudocode to paper, and moves pluralistic alignment toward practical needs.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Conclusion</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This work establishes a theoretical foundation for viewing preference alignment as a problem of preference reconstruction. Inspired by revealed preference theory, our approach formalizes alignment as a two-stage process: first, constructing a functional basis of proxy agents using attribute-guided prompting; and then, recovering aggregation weights through supervised learning. In doing so, our work offers the first tractable implementation of pluralistic alignment, along with quantitative metrics to assess both diversity and fidelity.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Beyond its theoretical contributions, this framework offers a practical platform for interdisciplinary research. In the social sciences, it can support survey design, question testing, and nonresponse mitigation, serving as a cost-effective complement to traditional data collection. For researchers in LLM alignment, the modularity of our system provides a controlled setting to evaluate prompt engineering strategies and their influence on agent diversity and downstream performance. More broadly, it lays the groundwork for agentic systems that enable value-sensitive modeling across disciplines. Notably, <span id="S5.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">P2P</span> repositions social survey data not as training material, but as a guiding signal for culturally grounded model alignment.</p>
</div>
</section>
